@node Overview
@chapter Overview

Sake is a build system designed to replace classical Makefile-based tools 
with declarative GNU Guile Scheme code. Instead of relying on a custom, 
macro-heavy DSL, Sake leverages Scheme procedures to construct a Directed 
Acyclic Graph (DAG) of build targets and their dependencies.

Key concepts in Sake:
@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Target}: A named goal, usually corresponding to an output file or a pseudo-target.
@item
@strong{Dependencies}: A list of files or other targets that must be up to date before the target is processed.
@item
@strong{Recipe}: A shell command executed to update the target when any dependency is newer than the target file.
@end itemize

@node Scheme Quickstart
@chapter Scheme Quickstart for Sake Users

You do @strong{not} need prior knowledge of Scheme or functional programming to write @file{sakefile.scm} files. Sake uses a tiny subset of Scheme.

@section Parentheses and Procedure Calls

In Scheme, operations are written inside parentheses, with the action/procedure first:

@example
;; In C/Shell: cc = "gcc"
;; In Scheme(sake):
(sakevar cc "gcc")

;; Calling a procedure: (procedure-name arg1 arg2)
(target "main.o" #:deps '("main.c") #:cmd "gcc -c main.c -o main.o")
@end example

@section Data Types Used in Sake

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Strings}: Enclosed in double quotes, e.g., @code{"gcc"} or @code{"main.o"}.
@item
@strong{Lists}: A collection of items preceded by a single quote, e.g., @code{'("file1.c" "file2.c")} or @code{'()}.
@item
@strong{Keywords}: Words starting with @code{#:}, used for named arguments like @code{#:deps} and @code{#:cmd}.
@end itemize

@node Mapping Make Concepts
@chapter Mapping GNU Make Concepts to Sake

Users familiar with GNU Make can translate their build concepts directly into Sake:

@multitable @columnfractions .30 .70
@headitem GNU Make Concept @tab Sake Equivalent
@item Variable Definition (@code{CC = gcc}) @tab @code{(define cc "gcc")}
@item Conditional Default (@code{CFLAGS ?= -O2}) @tab @code{(sakevar cflags "-O2")}
@item Default Goal (@code{all: main}) @tab First defined @code{(target "all" ...)}
@item Phony Target (@code{.PHONY: clean}) @tab Regular target with non-existent file output
@item Pattern Rule (@code{%.o: %.c}) @tab Scheme loop using @code{for-each} or @code{map}
@item Automatic Variables (@code{\(@@}, @code{\)<}) @tab String formatting via @code{(format #f ...)}
@end multitable

@node Writing Sakefiles
@chapter Writing Sakefiles

A @file{sakefile.scm} is an executable Scheme script interpreted by GNU Guile during the Sake build pass.

Standard Scheme definitions, variables, and Guile string formatting utilities (@code{format}, @code{string-join}, @code{map}) are fully accessible inside a @file{sakefile.scm} to assemble build parameters dynamically.

@node Environment Variables
@chapter Environment Variables and @code{sakevar}

In traditional Makefiles, variables automatically inherit values from shell environment variables with default fallbacks (e.g., @code{CC ?= gcc}).

To keep syntax concise and avoid verbose calls to @code{or} and @code{getenv}, Sake provides the @code{sakevar} macro.

@deffn {Macro} sakevar variable default-value
Binds @var{variable} to the value of its corresponding uppercase shell environment variable. If the environment variable is unset or empty, it falls back to @var{default-value}.
@end deffn

For example:

@example
(sakevar cc "gcc")
(sakevar cflags "-O2 -Wall")
(sakevar ldflags "-lncursesw")
@end example

This macro automatically inspects the environment for @env{CC}, @env{CFLAGS}, and @env{LDFLAGS}, expanding at compile time to:

@example
(define cc (or (getenv "CC") "gcc"))
(define cflags (or (getenv "CFLAGS") "-O2 -Wall"))
(define ldflags (or (getenv "LDFLAGS") "-lncursesw"))
@end example

@node Rules and Targets
@chapter Rules and Targets

@section The @code{target} Procedure

The primary primitive in Sake is the @code{target} procedure. It registers a node in the internal build graph.

@deffn {Scheme Procedure} target name [#:deps deps] [#:cmd cmd]
Registers a build target named @var{name}.

@itemize @bullet
@item
@var{name}: A string naming the target file or abstract goal (e.g., @code{"all"}).
@item
@var{#:deps}: A list of strings representing file or target dependencies. Defaults to @code{'()}.
@item
@var{#:cmd}: A string containing the shell command to execute when the target is out of date. Defaults to @code{""}.
@end itemize
@end deffn

@section Dependency Evaluation

Sake evaluates target modification times against its dependencies. A target is considered out of date and recompiled if:
@enumerate
@item
The target file does not exist on disk.
@item
Any file listed in @var{#:deps} has a newer timestamp than the target file.
@item
A dependency is a pseudo-target that was executed in the current run.
@end enumerate

@node Pattern Rules
@chapter Pattern Rules and Batch Generation

In GNU Make, pattern rules use implicit match syntax like @code{%.o: %.c}. In Sake, pattern generation is expressed procedurally using Guile's @code{for-each} procedure.

@example
(define sources '("main" "utils" "parser"))

;; Generate rules for compiling each .c file into a .o file
(for-each
  (lambda (name)
    (let ((c-file (format #f "~a.c" name))
          (o-file (format #f "~a.o" name)))
      (target o-file
              #:deps (list c-file)
              #:cmd (format #f "~a ~a -c ~a -o ~a" cc cflags c-file o-file))))
  sources)
@end example

This explicit approach eliminates implicit rule resolution bugs while retaining the concise nature of pattern rules.

@node Abstract Targets
@chapter Abstract (Phony) Targets

In GNU Make, targets that do not produce output files require a special @code{.PHONY} declaration. In Sake, abstract goals such as @code{"clean"} or @code{"install"} require no special syntax. Because their corresponding files do not exist on disk, Sake automatically considers them out of date and executes their recipe command whenever invoked.

@example
(target "clean"
        #:deps '()
        #:cmd "rm -f *.o my_program")
@end example

@node Practical Example
@chapter Practical Example: Building GNU Nano

Below is a complete @file{sakefile.scm} used to compile @code{GNU Nano}:

@example
(sakevar cc "gcc")
(sakevar cflags "-O2 -Wall -I. -I.. -I../lib -DHAVE_CONFIG_H -DLOCALEDIR=\\\"/usr/local/share/locale\\\" -DSYSCONFDIR=\\\"/etc\\\"")
(sakevar ldflags "-lncursesw -lmagic")

(define nano-sources
  '("browser" "chars" "color" "cut" "files" "global"
    "help" "history" "move" "nano" "prompt" "rcfile"
    "search" "text" "utils" "winio"))

(define nano-objects
  (map (lambda (name) (format #f "~a.o" name)) nano-sources))

(target "all"
        #:deps '("nano")
        #:cmd "")

(target "revision.h"
        #:deps '()
        #:cmd "echo '#define REVISION \"git-build\"' | cmp -s - revision.h || echo '#define REVISION \"git-build\"' > revision.h")

(target "../lib/libgnu.a"
        #:deps '()
        #:cmd "make -C ../lib")

(for-each
  (lambda (name)
    (let ((c-file (format #f "~a.c" name))
          (o-file (format #f "~a.o" name)))
      (target o-file
              #:deps (list c-file "revision.h" "../config.h" "../lib/libgnu.a")
              #:cmd (format #f "~a ~a -c ~a -o ~a" cc cflags c-file o-file))))
  nano-sources)

(target "nano"
        #:deps (cons "../lib/libgnu.a" nano-objects)
        #:cmd (format #f "~a -o nano ~a ../lib/libgnu.a ~a"
                      cc
                      (string-join nano-objects " ")
                      ldflags))

(target "install-symlink"
        #:deps '("nano")
        #:cmd "ln -sf nano rnano")
@end example

@node Invocation
@chapter Invocation

Sake is invoked from the command line in the directory containing @file{sakefile.scm}:

@example
sake [TARGET]
@end example

If no @var{TARGET} is specified, Sake defaults to building the first registered target (usually @code{"all"}).

